# Story: A fleet of agents and the board they report to (209s)
# Hook: Four Claude agents build a web app in one session — and every one of them reports to a shared board.
# Duration: 209s
# Source: ../out/agent-board-fleet.mp4  (take 1, 2026-08-15, 209.4s)
#
# Voiceover script with timing cues.
# Format: [START --> END] narration text
# Gaps between entries are silent — the viewer is reading the board.
#
# HOW THESE CUES WERE DERIVED. Not from the tape's Sleep lines: VHS drops frames
# unevenly under load, so tape time is not video time. They were read off the
# FRAMES — six crops of the board pane at t=25/50/75/120/160/200 (_run/contact.png),
# each giving the newest post visible and therefore a wall-clock bracket. Two of
# those brackets are one post wide, and together they fix the mapping at
#
#     video_t  =  wall_clock - 15:13:47
#
# which happens to be 1:1 here: this take was captured in real time. Every cue
# below is a board timestamp put through that line. If the video is ever
# re-recorded, these cues are wrong until they are re-derived the same way.
#
# BUDGET: windows are sized at 11-13 characters per second. OpenAI nova at
# speed 1.0 runs about 15 ch/s, so every line has headroom. This matters more
# than it looks — tts_openai.py advances its cursor by the ACTUAL audio length,
# so one overflowing segment shifts every segment after it, and the drift is
# silent.
#
# Total segments: 16

[00:00.5 --> 00:07.5]
One rysh session. In a moment, four Claude agents will go to work inside it.

[00:08.0 --> 00:15.5]
Three lanes: planning on the left, the builders in the middle, the shared board on the right.

[00:18.5 --> 00:26.0]
The roadmap agent goes first, and its very first act is to tell the board what this demo is.

[00:26.5 --> 00:34.0]
Then the other three check in. Standing by. Nobody has been given any work yet.

[00:35.0 --> 00:45.0]
The roadmap writes the goal down: a todo manager that runs entirely in the browser. No backend, no build step, no dependencies.

[00:47.0 --> 00:56.0]
It posts the deliverable, then hands the whole thing to the fleet manager. That is the last order it gives.

[01:00.0 --> 01:10.0]
The manager splits the work by layer. Worker one takes the markup and the styling, worker two takes all of the logic.

[01:12.0 --> 01:24.0]
But first it writes a contract: the six element ids both halves must agree on. That is what stops two parallel agents from colliding.

[01:26.0 --> 01:36.0]
Contract posted. Now the work orders go out, one to each worker, and the build starts.

[01:38.0 --> 01:50.0]
Watch the middle lane. Both workers are running at once, and neither has read the other's file. The contract is the only thing holding them together.

[01:58.0 --> 02:08.0]
Worker one is done: the page and the stylesheet, checked back against the contract, id by id.

[02:25.0 --> 02:37.0]
Worker two finishes the logic. Add, toggle, delete, clear completed, a live count, and the list persisted to local storage.

[02:42.0 --> 02:56.0]
Notice that neither worker reported back by message. They posted to the board, and the manager has been watching it, because a post cannot be missed and a message can.

[02:57.0 --> 03:10.0]
Now the manager verifies for itself. Every id lines up, the page loads both files, the syntax is clean. Then it signs off.

[03:13.0 --> 03:25.0]
The roadmap checks the work against its own acceptance list and closes the demo. Four agents, one board, a working app.

[03:25.5 --> 03:29.0]
And all four have signed off.
